    licence: 'CC0',
    note: 'The nearest thing to black either library has, and nearly featureless with it: a step of 0.97 against the oak\'s 5.39. It paints the quietest desk of anything here, at a swing of 1.05 where the drawn boards swing 2.0.',
  },
];

/* Treatments, not scans. Same recipe both times: pull most of the chroma out, then drop the
   level with a gain and an offset rather than a brightness multiply, which keeps the grain's
   own step instead of losing it to 8-bit rounding and to WebP on a flat dark patch. */
const TREATED: readonly Tile[] = [
  {
    file: 'oak-7760-smoked',
    name: 'Slip Match Oak, smoked',
    weight: '122 KB',
    L: '41.3',
    C: '5.3',
    step: '3.76',
    ratio: '0.755',
    source: 'Poliigon 7760, treated',
    href: 'https://www.poliigon.com/texture/slip-match-oak-wood-veneer-texture/7760',
    licence: 'Poliigon Free',
    note: 'The levelled tile you nearly picked, 13 points of lightness darker with three quarters of its chroma taken out. Same cathedral figure, no red left in it. Superseded: the painting sets the tone now, and this cut reaches the desk with less grain than the tile it was cut from.',
  },
  {
    file: 'cc0-oak-light-smoked',
    name: 'Oak Veneer 02, smoked',
    weight: '111 KB',
    L: '40.9',
    C: '5.0',
    step: '5.33',
    ratio: '1.212',
    source: 'Poly Haven, treated',
    href: 'https://polyhaven.com/a/oak_veneer_02',
    licence: 'CC0',
    note: 'The same treatment on the sharper grain, landing within half a point of the tile above on both lightness and chroma. Fine straight lines where the other has figure, so the pair is a clean choice of grain and nothing else.',
  },
  {
    file: 'oak-7760-ebonised',
    name: 'Slip Match Oak, ebonised',
    weight: '118 KB',
    L: '20.1',
    C: '4.7',
    step: '3.47',
    ratio: '0.746',
    source: 'Poliigon 7760, treated',
    href: 'https://www.poliigon.com/texture/slip-match-oak-wood-veneer-texture/7760',
    licence: 'Poliigon Free',
    note: 'Near black and the figure still reads: the step holds at 3.47, where taking the same tile to this level with a brightness multiply drops it to 0.65.',
  },
  {
    file: 'cc0-oak-light-ebonised',
    name: 'Oak Veneer 02, ebonised',
    weight: '106 KB',
    L: '20.1',
    C: '4.3',
    step: '5.02',
    ratio: '1.212',
    source: 'Poly Haven, treated',
    href: 'https://polyhaven.com/a/oak_veneer_02',
    licence: 'CC0',
    note: 'The same lightness as the tile above to one decimal place, on the sharper grain. Cut when the file had to carry the tone; now that the desk does, it is the losses on the way down that show.',
  },
];

const GROUPS = [
  {
    title: 'Poliigon, round 2',
    tiles: POLIIGON,
    lead: 'The two free Poliigon scans, cut in round 2 and untouched since.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Poly Haven, untreated',
    tiles: CC0,
    lead: 'Poly Haven releases everything as CC0, which is public domain: any purpose, no credit needed, redistribution allowed. These are the colour maps as downloaded, with nothing done to them but the cut.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Treated for the dark themes',
    tiles: TREATED,
    lead: 'Cut when the file had to be as dark as the desk, because no library gives away a wood that is near black and still shows grain. The painting reaches that black from any tile now, so these four are kept for the record rather than in the running. Each is named after the scan it comes from.',
  },
] as const;

const ALL = [...POLIIGON, ...CC0, ...TREATED];
const tileByFile = Object.fromEntries(ALL.map((tile) => [tile.file, tile]));

/* The four left standing for dark and forest. All four now paint at the same near black, so
   the only thing left to choose between them is the grain. Swing is the painted desk's L*
   standard deviation, read off the bare margin beside the sheets at 1440x900. The drawn boards
   this would replace measure 2.02 there, and the old painting left these tiles at 0.3 to 0.6. */
const SHORTLIST = [
  {
    file: 'ash-4186',
    swing: '3.11',
    figure:
      'Sawn boards, plank joints and all. The same wood light and arctic are settled on, so all four desks would be one board.',
  },
  {
    file: 'oak-7760-mid',
    swing: '2.45',
    figure:
      'Cathedral figure across the page and no joint anywhere in it. Nearest of the four to the drawn boards it replaces.',
  },
  {
    file: 'cc0-oak-light',
    swing: '2.09',
    figure: 'Fine straight lines, no figure and no joints. The quiet one.',
  },
  {
    file: 'cc0-oak-grey',
    swing: '4.14',
    figure:
      'Open ring-porous oak, coarse and streaky. Twice the swing of the quiet one and the only one of the four you can read from across the room.',
  },
] as const;

const SHORT_FILES = SHORTLIST.map((tile) => tile.file);

/* Round 5. The dark desk keeps the ash and only the polish is open, so these are the settings
   of it, measured the way the shortlist was: L* per pixel off the bare margin beside the
   sheets at 1440x900. Body is the median, top the 99th percentile, black the share of the
   margin under L* 1, and sheen the share above L* 17, which is where round 4's desk stopped. */
const POLISH = [
  {
    name: 'None, as round 4 left it',
    gloss: '',
    levels: 'no floor, no gain',
    mean: '10.0',
    body: '10.2',
    top: '16.0',
    black: '0.0',
    sheen: '0.1',
    note: 'Dark wood at an even lightness, the grain legible the whole way across the margin.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Satin',
    gloss: 'satin',
    levels: '239 / 112',
    mean: '7.9',
    body: '7.2',
    top: '18.8',
    black: '8.6',
    sheen: '3.7',
    note: 'Still wood. The dark grain closes up, the light fibres stand out of it, and a twelfth of the margin has gone black.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Polished',
    gloss: 'polished',
    levels: '235 / 135',
    mean: '6.5',
    body: '5.4',
    top: '19.3',
    black: '18.2',
    sheen: '3.6',
    note: 'Black between the fibres, figure lit. A sixth of the margin is black and the top end has passed where round 4 had it.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Lacquer',
    gloss: 'lacquer',
    levels: '231 / 166',
    mean: '5.5',
    body: '3.6',
    top: '21.1',
    black: '33.3',
    sheen: '5.7',
    note: 'A third of the margin is flat black and the sheen carries the grain by itself. The loudest of the three.',
  },
] as const;

const THEMES = [
  { id: 'light', label: 'Light', try: ['ash-4186', 'cc0-oak-grey', 'oak-7760'], noVeilLink: true },
  { id: 'arctic', label: 'Arctic', try: ['ash-4186', 'cc0-oak-grey', 'oak-7760-mid'], noVeilLink: true },
  { id: 'dark', label: 'Dark', try: SHORT_FILES, noVeilLink: false },
  { id: 'dark-forest', label: 'Dark forest', try: SHORT_FILES, noVeilLink: false },
] as const;

const deskUrl = (file: string, theme: string, veil: boolean) =>
  `../?desk=${file}&theme=${theme}${veil ? '' : '&veil=0'}`;

const glossUrl = (gloss: string) => `../?desk=ash-4186&theme=dark${gloss ? `&gloss=${gloss}` : ''}`;
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex" />
    <title>Desk wood, candidates</title>
    <style is:inline>
      :root {
        color-scheme: light dark;
        --ink: #1b1a17;
        --paper: #f6f3ee;
        --faint: #6b6560;
        --rule: #d8d1c7;
      }

      @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
        :root {
          --ink: #ece7df;
          --paper: #171614;
          --faint: #9a938a;
          --rule: #35322d;
        }
      }

      body {
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 2rem 1rem 6rem;
        max-width: 54rem;
        background: var(--paper);
        color: var(--ink);
        font: 1rem/1.6 system-ui, sans-serif;
      }

      h1 {
        font-size: 1.5rem;
        margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
      }

      h2 {
        font-size: 1.125rem;
        margin: 2.5rem 0 0.5rem;
        padding-top: 1rem;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
      }

      h3 {
        font-size: 1rem;
        margin: 1.75rem 0 0.25rem;
      }

      p {
        margin: 0.25rem 0 0.75rem;
      }

      .lede,
      .note {
        color: var(--faint);
      }

      .strip {
        height: 11rem;
        border: 1px solid var(--rule);
        border-radius: 0.25rem;
        background-repeat: repeat;
        background-size: 45rem 45rem;
      }

      .links {
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        gap: 0.25rem 1rem;
        margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
        padding: 0;
        list-style: none;
      }

      .links li {
        white-space: nowrap;
      }

      .scroll {
        overflow-x: auto;
      }

      table {
        width: 100%;
        margin: 1rem 0 0;
        border-collapse: collapse;
        font-size: 0.9375rem;
      }

      th,
      td {
        padding: 0.35rem 0.75rem 0.35rem 0;
        text-align: left;
        vertical-align: top;
        white-space: nowrap;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
      }

      th {
        font-weight: 600;
      }

      th.num,
      td.num {
        text-align: right;
        padding-right: 1rem;
        font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
      }

      td:not(:first-child) {
        color: var(--faint);
      }

      code {
        font-family: ui-monospace, monospace;
        font-size: 0.875em;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Desk wood, candidates</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every tile cut so far. The links at the foot of the page open the CV with that tile on the
      desk and the theme already picked. A light desk takes the tile under a 15 per cent veil of
      its own colour and has a second link that lifts the veil; a dark desk subtracts the tile
      instead, so there the veil is the only thing holding the colour and there is nothing to
      lift.
    </p>
    <p class="lede">
      Light is settled on the ash, arctic and forest on the levelled oak, and dark on the ash
      as well. The only thing still open is how far the dark desk is polished, which is the
      section near the foot of the page.
    </p>

    <h2>How to read the numbers</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      L* is mean lightness on 0 to 100 and C* is mean chroma, both in CIELAB, and both describe
      the file rather than the desk. Step is the mean absolute luminance step between
      neighbouring rows of pixels on 0 to 255, which is across the grain on every tile but{' '}
      <code>oak-7760-down</code>, and step over the tile's own standard deviation says how much
      of that step is fibre rather than figure. Higher is sharper both times. What reaches the
      desk is a different number, and the shortlist below carries it. Every tile is 1440 px
      square and paints at 45rem, so one image pixel lands on one device pixel at 2x. Painting
      it smaller costs grain: at 22.5rem the same tile loses a third of its swing.
    </p>

    {GROUPS.map((group) => (
      <>
        <h2>{group.title}</h2>
        <p class="lede">{group.lead}</p>
        <div class="scroll">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Tile</th>
                <th>File</th>
                <th class="num">L*</th>
                <th class="num">C*</th>
                <th class="num">Step</th>
                <th class="num">Step/sd</th>
                <th>Source</th>
                <th>Licence</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {group.tiles.map((tile) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{tile.name}</td>
                  <td><code>{tile.file}.webp</code>, {tile.weight}</td>
                  <td class="num">{tile.L}</td>
                  <td class="num">{tile.C}</td>
                  <td class="num">{tile.step}</td>
                  <td class="num">{tile.ratio}</td>
                  <td><a href={tile.href}>{tile.source}</a></td>
                  <td>{tile.licence}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        {group.tiles.map((tile) => (
          <>
            <h3>{tile.name}</h3>
            <p class="note">{tile.note} <code>{tile.file}.webp</code></p>
            <div class="strip" style={`background-image: url(../desk/${tile.file}.webp)`} />
          </>
        ))}
      </>
    ))}

    <h2>Dark and forest, down to four</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      The grain was invisible on the dark desks because of how the tile was painted, not because
      of anything in the tile. The desk blended the tile with overlay against its own colour,
      and overlay scales every step the tile has by base times one minus base: strongest on a
      mid tone, gone by the time the base is near black. The painted desk was swinging 0.3 to
      0.6 L* where the drawn boards swing 2.0, so all that came through was the tone, which is
      why three tiles with nothing in common looked alike.
    </p>
    <p class="lede">
      The dark desks now subtract the tile from a base cut to the tile's own mean instead. A
      subtraction keeps every step at its full size however dark the result lands, and the veil
      goes opaque and blends with color, which hands the desk back the theme's own hue and
      chroma. Painted lightness is L* 10 and chroma 6.2 on dark, 10.5 on forest, within half a
      point of the drawn boards on both. Every candidate below lands there, whatever it was cut
      at, so nothing but the grain is left to choose.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Tile</th>
            <th>File</th>
            <th class="num">Swing</th>
            <th>What it looks like</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {SHORTLIST.map((tile) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{tileByFile[tile.file].name}</td>
              <td><code>{tile.file}.webp</code></td>
              <td class="num">{tile.swing}</td>
              <td style="white-space: normal">{tile.figure}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="lede">
      The four smoked and ebonised cuts are off the list. Their whole job was to carry the dark
      tone in the file, and the painting does that now, so all they add is the grain they lost
      on the way down. <code>cc0-black-flamed</code> is off it too, at a swing of 1.05: it is
      there if you want the desk quieter than the drawn boards rather than louder.
    </p>

    <h2>Dark, blacker, the light grain as gloss</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      The dark desk keeps the ash. What is open is how far down its low end goes. Two layers
      now sit between the tile and the veil. A flat grey under color-burn clips to black every
      fibre more than a set distance below the tile's own mean, and a second flat grey under
      color-dodge multiplies whatever survived back up. Clip, then open up: the range widens
      downwards instead of sliding, so the body of the desk goes black while the bright fibres
      hold the lightness they had or pass it. A plain offset would have taken the highlights
      down with everything else.
    </p>
    <p class="lede">
      The two numbers under each name are those greys, floor first. Both live in the painting,
      so no tile file changed and the other three desks are byte for byte what they were.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Polish</th>
            <th>Floor / gain</th>
            <th class="num">Mean</th>
            <th class="num">Body</th>
            <th class="num">Top</th>
            <th class="num">Black</th>
            <th class="num">Sheen</th>
            <th>What it looks like</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {POLISH.map((polish) => (
            <tr>
              <td><a href={glossUrl(polish.gloss)}>{polish.name}</a></td>
              <td>{polish.levels}</td>
              <td class="num">{polish.mean}</td>
              <td class="num">{polish.body}</td>
              <td class="num">{polish.top}</td>
              <td class="num">{polish.black}%</td>
              <td class="num">{polish.sheen}%</td>
              <td style="white-space: normal">{polish.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="lede">
      Sheen is a small share of the desk by design. A highlight that takes 4 per cent of the
      area and stands 14 L* over the body reads as light caught on something hard; the same
      lightness spread evenly reads as grey wood, which is what the drawn boards do. Chroma
      splits with it. The dark half of the margin falls from C* 5.2 to between 0 and 2.5,
      because there is no room for colour that near black, while the brightest twentieth stays
      at 6.1, so the desk's brown now lives in the sheen and nowhere else.
    </p>
    <h3><a href={glossUrl('window')}>Window</a></h3>
    <p class="lede">
      Polished, plus a broad reflection: the gain climbs from grey 105 on the left of the desk
      to 160 on the right instead of holding at 135, so the far side of the page catches more
      light. It runs left to right and never up or down, which is the only reason it survives
      the sheets. A gradient with any vertical run would be cut into steps by them, and every
      strip of bare desk would show a different piece of it. This one shows the same sweep in
      every strip. The right margin reads mean 8.3, body 7.2, top 23.4 and 12 per cent sheen;
      the left reads 5.7, 5.0, 16.0 and 0.3 per cent. That is the same desk at both ends, lit
      from one side.
    </p>

    <h2>On the desk</h2>
    {THEMES.map((theme) => (
      <>
        <h3>{theme.label}</h3>
        <ul class="links">
          {theme.try.map((file) => (
            <li>
              <a href={deskUrl(file, theme.id, true)}>{tileByFile[file].name}</a>
              {theme.noVeilLink ? <> <a href={deskUrl(file, theme.id, false)}>(no veil)</a></> : null}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </>
    ))}

    <h2>If none of them work</h2>
    <p>
      Tone and grain are separate knobs now. The desk's lightness is one number in the painting,
      so darker or lighter than the L* 10 the shortlist paints at is a one-line change and any
      tile can follow it. How loud the grain is comes from the tile, and the shortlist spans 2.1
      to 4.1 against the drawn boards' 2.0, so say louder or quieter and I will say which tile
      that is.
    </p>
  </body>
</html>
